From: Nikola Milutinovic <Nikola.Milutinovic@ev.co.yu>
Subject: Re: RAID 0+1 faster than RAID 5 ? How much faster?
Date: 1999/02/24
Message-ID: <36D3CF6C.899456EA@ev.co.yu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 447832903
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <7av07j$hqn$1@charm.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
X-Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: EPS JP Elektrovojvodina
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Steen Hansen wrote:
> 
> I have a RAID 310 disk array, configured for RAID 5. It is used in a mixed
> read and write environment, with a lot of random traffic.
> 
> I have read a number of places that RAID 0+1 works faster, for these reasons:
> 
>  - Less overhead for writes
>  - Reads can be done alternating between the two halfs of the mirror, in
>    effect doubling the capacity
> 
> I have not seen any data indicating HOW much faster one can expect it to work.
> Sure, it depends a lot on how they are used, but some anecdotes would be
> nice to hear.

RAID 5
------

Requires n + 1 disks. For every n stripes (stripe = m * block), there is an
additional CHKsum stripe. All these n+1 stripes are writen to all n+1 disks, as
paralelly, as SCSI configuration would permit. If all disks have separate SCSI
channels, then it can be done totally in paralel.

Requires processing power for that CHKsum operation - usually in a form of a
special controller card. For instance, we have Mylex RAID and it has a RISC
microprocessor on it, Intel i960.

RAID 0
------

Requres n disks. Performs a simple stripping.

RAID 1
------

Requires 2 disks. Performs mirroring. Usually, doesn't require awsome computing
power.

RAID 0+1
--------

Requires 2 * n disks. Performs stripping upon mirrored pairs of disks. Again,
not a processor hog.

--------

So, it looks like RAID 0+1 is faster than RAID 5.

But, think again. All the overhead of RAID 5 is USUALLY handled by a specially
designed RAID controller, which should have enough computing power on it.
Remember, disk operations are much slower than memory/processor operations. Even
if you have all disks on separate SCSI channels, the on-board processor should
be fast enough.

The only advantage I can see in RAID 0+1 is that it can theoretically survive n
disk failures, where RAID 5 can survive only 1. Of course, if both disks from a
disk pair in RAID 0+1 crash, you're gone. Speed can be greatly achieved through
sufficient on-controller cache.

We have Mylex 3-channel RAID SCSI controller in RAID 5 scheme and we perform
monthly backups on DAT.



