David Bentzen wrote:
> 
> Hello all,
> 
> I have a DEC Alphaserver 1000 4/266 running DEC Unix 4.0e.  I need to
> install another SCSI controller.  The existing scsi controller is
> identified as an NCR53C10.  I think this is the controller chip on the
> board itself.
> 
> Without opening the machine up to get to the hardware, I cannot
> determine who the manufacturer of the board is.
> 
> Without opening up the machine is there a way to tell who the
> manufacturer of the board is?

It's most likely a DEC card (if it is card and not built-in to the
system board itself), especially if that is what was installed "at the
factory". The built-in and PCI SCSI adapters on my AlphaServer 2000
report as NCR53C810.

> OR
> 
> Can anyone recommend a PCI SCSI card that will work with DEC Unix?
> 

First thing I would suggest is checking out the options and upgrade
catalog (http://www.digital.com/alphaserver/tech_docs/alphasrv1000/ if
you do not have it handy). See what "supported" cards and upgrades are
recommended for your machine by DECompaq. Hopefully, some other people
can provide "compatible" equivalents.

Otherwise, this depends on what you need. I installed a DEC KZPAA
PCI/SCSI card on my AlphaServer 2000 which currently runs DUNIX 4.0d
(will work on AlphaServer 1000 as low as DUNIX 3.0B according to the
catalog). It is a single port, "generic" SCSI card. It worked
immediately after installation/reboot. I run it and the built-in
controller simultaneously without problem. If you want a
multi-port/hardware RAID/ULTA SCSI, you will probably need a different
card.

Hope that helps...

