both postings from Sergey and Jeff are missing the point.
the answer is simple:
when a parent forks a child, the child does an exit (for whatever reason, this may also be an abnormal exit) but the parent doesn't wait(2) on it, the kernel holds the accounting information for the child.
as soon as the parent do one of the wait variants, the kernel releases the PCB for the child and you wont see the defunct anymore.
a defunct is not annoying so much, it doesn't take system resources, except for a slot in the (limited) array of tasks.
if  you encounter a pbl with "no more processes", max_task is reached.
you can work around that by setting maxusers higher and wait for your vendor to fix the pbl.

now for some pedagogy, first lets handle Sergey:
you suggest:

> Try to
> # ps -fe | grep 10710
> # kill 10710
>

did you try your idea? it won't work. so what's the point in making a posting?
i saw a similar posting for a question regarding "truss" on DUX: someone says "try one of the CDs" in reply to someone who asks on which CD he can find truss. the latter mentions he searched them...

then Jeff:

> ....
>
> Outside the realm of vampires, you can't kill something that's
> already dead, even a zombie process.  That defunct process
> still has a PID and a parent, but that's about it.

well, defunct is EXACTLY a zombie. one is legacy unix term, the other is slang...

>
> If the parent PID of these defunct processes is 1 (the init process),
