you've already got  a bunch of savvy answers, i would like to add some perspective help:
you say "the system is horribly slow for compiling". that leaves you plenty of time to analyze what's going on.
you don't mention how many disks (if more than 1) and of what type, neither how much mem.
one guy responded, and that's exact, compiling involves heavy IO.
try running "top" (3.4, you'll find it on the freeware CD), "monitor" (1.6, same location) or "iostat"
this will tell you if your compilation is rather IO or CPU bound. watch the "idle cpu" figures, this will tell.

then you'll probably find out you have only 1 disk, 24MB of memory, a lot of swapping activity, while all disk and paging activity goes to the same narrow SCSI controller...
if that's your case, a 2nd cpu will not help much.

if you can get a machine with a lot of mem (a lot means no swapping takes place when compling), try this trick:
define a memory fs of a few megs and set the TEMP env var (in your profile) to this mfs. IO will be eliminated, increasing perf considerably.

