[adding to Jeff's reply]

So it's been "fixed" in V4.0E and later releases.  You can upgrade the OS,
use Jeff's workaround, or rename "readonly" in your source code to avoid
the problem.  There's a fourth solution you might also want to consider.
The Tru64 UNIX development environment team is now field-testing an update
kit for certain development tools (called the "DTK" or Developers Toolkit).

The first field test update kit (DTK EFT1) contains the DEC C compiler and
installs and runs on V4.0D and V4.0E systems.  It currently provides the
following new C compiler features:

    o  parallel programming support (both the OpenMP parallel programming API

       and old-style SGI manual decomposition directives)

    o  major source_listing improvements

    o  optional dynamic array bounds checking

    o  variable length arrays, restricted pointers, initializers with
       designators, and other new C9X features

    o  significant new diagnostic controls (pragmas and command-line options)

    o  new compiler diagnostics (detection of undefined side-effects,
       unnecessary #include files, potential 64-bit gotchas, etc.)

    o  enhanced Microsoft compatibility mode (including thread local storage)

    o  significant optimization improvements (including improved utilization
       of new alpha hardware, enhanced intrinsic function support, better
loop
       transforms, etc.)

DTK EFT2 should ship soon and may also include new versions of decladebug,
the atom-based program analysis tools, and the Visual Threads package.  It
should install and run on V4.0D, V4.0E, and V4.0F.

The DTK field test kits are down-loadable from the web and simple to
install.  They do not remove access to your current tools.  And they're free
to all those with a current Tru64 UNIX development license.

If you're interested, send mail to Pat Mulvey at mulvey@zko.dec.com to
sign-up for the field test.

\John Parks


