On Fri, 12 Feb 1999 11:06:38 -0600, Scott Risdal <srisdal@superior.dul.epa.gov> wrote:
>Our group is currently using an Alpha 3000/175 workstation class system
>running Unix 4.0d and C++ 6.x.  The system is used for purposes of C++
>software development and is horribly slow for compiling.
>
>Our client has offered to provide us with one of their other older
>machines, a 2100-4/275 containing a single CPU.  My questions:
>
>1) Can we expect any significant improvement in performance on this
>machine?

Yes.  I have 5 2100s, 1 3000/800, and 1 4100/533.  The 2100s blow the
doors off the 3000/800[1] (by the same token the performance of the 4100
is far better than the 2100s).  Most of the gain is from cpu speed and
architecture differences (the 4/275 is a 4 stage processor and, IIRC,
the 3000 uses a 2 stage processor).  In the case the the 3000/800 the
TurboChannel bus is about the same speed as the 2100's PCI bus.

>2) Can we upgrade the CPU to faster one?

Yes, but it's 'hard'.  Faster CPU's for the 2100s are getting harder
and harder to come by (memory cards are the same way -- IIRC, I can get
a 2100 for ~$3000 with no memory, but it costs $5000-8000 for a 512Meg
memory card).  I would suggest adding a second or a third processor, as
the 4/275's are very reasonably priced these days.


>3) Could we significantly improve performance by adding a second CPU?

Yes, though you don't get the 100% performance increase that some
people expect.  It's closer to 75 to 80%.

>4) Does the C++ compiler take advantage of  multiple CPU environments?

Dec says it does.  cmp(1) tells me it doesn't make much of a difference.


[1] Quite literally.  I have to keep the plastic door covers taped to
the sheet metal doors on the side of the 3000 tower, as they have come
loose over the years.




