Even if the compiler itself doesn't take much advantage of multiple
CPU:s, you can still gain a lot when compiling large projects by
compiling multiple C files at the same time by using a parallell make
such as GNU make (look at the '-j' flag). I haven't checked if the
standard make supplied by DEC provides such functionality.

Note: Compiling and linking lots of stuff tends to produce quite heavy
disk I/O. If just one CPU spends most of its time waiting for disk
I/O, a second CPU won't help much help. A nice aspect of that problem
is that both fast disks and fast controllers to go with them are
relatively cheap.

