In article <36C45F9D.6C4DEFE2@superior.dul.epa.gov>, Scott Risdal
<srisdal@superior.dul.epa.gov> writes
>Our client has offered to provide us with one of their other older
>machines, a 2100-4/275 containing a single CPU.  My questions:
>
>1) Can we expect any significant improvement in performance on this
>machine?

If I remember rightly, the 4/275 is a 21064A, and the older machines had
the 21064. There were some architectural differences which made the "A"
variant a fair bit faster.

You should get a significant boost but don't expect miracles - the
amount of memory, and the disks installed, will also be a factor to
consider.

>2) Can we upgrade the CPU to faster one?

From a 4/275, the next jump is a 5/250 or a 5/300 - there was also a
5/375 but these weren't very common.

The processors are on daughterboards, and they're becoming increasingly
difficult to find as production of the 2000 and 2100 series ended in
1997.

>3) Could we significantly improve performance by adding a second CPU?

Depends what you're trying to improve - but the 2100 can support 4 CPUs.
In a 4-CPU configuration, you can have two memory cards installed
(MS452-UA ?), yielding a maximum memory size of 1Gb. In a 1- or 2-CPU
setup, you can install four MS452's to achieve 2Gb maximum.

>4) Does the C++ compiler take advantage of  multiple CPU environments?

Supposedly yes, but both of our DU AlphaServers (300 4/266 and 800
5/333) are uniprocessor boxes so I can't say for sure !!!

Chris
