To: Jean-Damien DURAND <jdurand@pcfarm14.cern.ch>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Digital UNIX Development Environment, Compaq
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Hi,

Chris Forrest's answer is correct - this version of Third Degree
is showing you all the memory that was allocated but not free'd,
including that allocated by libpthread and libc.

The large amounts of memory are the thread stacks for the main
thread and for libpthread's internal utility threads, and the 
numerous smaller ones are a mutex pool, I think.

In any case, none of the stack traces mention your code, so don't
worry about them. The "leaks" that you are seeing are just places
where we have found it more efficient to not free the memory.
Furthermore, the only leaks that you really want to worry about 
are those that occur multiple times within one of your loops,
because freeing those avoids waste, whereas freeing just before
exit does not.

We realize that these non-leaks in system libraries can cause undue
concern and unwanted clutter, so the new version of Third Degree 
does not report them by default. 

	John P Williams, Unix Development Environment Group, Compaq



