Steen Hansen wrote:

> We have a purchased application running on our 4.0D version of Compaq's
> Unix (I refuse to call it the new stupid name).
>
> It leaves a stack of defunct processes sitting out there, which I would
> like to clean up, ahile we wait for the vendor to clean up his code. But I
> cannot kill them, I *believe* I can do that with other Unixes, though it's
> quite awhile ago I had any there.
>
> Why can't I kill this one?
>
> # ps -ef|grep 1018
> iii        1018  10710  0.0 -        ??           0:00.00 <defunct>
> root      19371   8041  0.2 16:48:27 ttyr1        0:00.02 grep 1018
> # kill 1018
> kill: 1018: no such process

Outside the realm of vampires, you can't kill something that's
already dead, even a zombie process.  That defunct process
still has a PID and a parent, but that's about it.

If the parent PID of these defunct processes is 1 (the init process),
then you've hit an OS bug -- init should be cleaning these up.

But if the parent isn't PID 1, then the only way to clean them up is
to kill the buggy parent, which isn't cleaning up its zombie children.

Most likely, this app was ported from a System V base, where it
used signal() to set SIGCHLD to SIG_IGN, which causes the system to
clean up zombies automatically.

Under Tru64 UNIX, formerly known as Digital UNIX, formerly know
as DEC OSF/1, formerly known as OSF/1, formerly known as
Mach 2.5, you have to use sigaction() with SA_FLAGS set to
SA_NOCLDWAIT for SIGCHLD to get the same automatic cleanup
behavior (or run in the UNIX 95 evironment using,
of course, the c89 compiler, or link with libsys5). Maybe you
can pass that on to your vendor.

