After I had just gotten used to the name "Digital UNIX" enough to call
it that to persons not employed by Digital, we get something that sounds
like something from a bad advertising campaign (hold the box up to the
camera while you're saying it).  I have already resolved to return to
calling it OSF/1 (not terribly descriptive, but at least dignified).

Does anyone really think that this or any other name change will do
anything to increase the commercial success of OSF/1?  I can think of no
case where an established company or product has been benefited by a
name change. In all cases I can think of, it has either been neutral or
derogratory (little-known companies or products are another matter).
The only circumstance under which I could conceive of such a thing being
beneficial is if a company purchased an inferior product with the
intention of making it over into something better (which is clearly not
the case here).  The most likely result is customer confusion, which is
of no benefit to anyone.

I am, of course, speaking on my own behalf.

