Hi Stan,

    I think it is possible to move /sbin to another disk and use a symbolic
link to access it.  But I do not recommend to do this because there is a lot
of system maintenance programs that you need to use in Single User Mode in
here.   You have to be careful about doing this; You should copy some
important commands out (mount, fsck and etc) into another directory in / and
move /sbin to the other file system however have to mount the file system
that keeps /sbin in single user mode to use the other files that you do not
copy in / filesystem.

    The growing of / size may come from many reasons. One that I suggest you
to check is whether /tmp use space of / file system or not.  It is possible
for users to put their files in here and make / file system full.    For
real cause of the problem, I suggest you to keep statistics of the growing
of each directory in / file system (may be by using "du -s -k $(ls -l | grep
^d| awk 'print $9')" or "du -s -k  (find / -mount -type d) ) so you can
identify which files or directories that keep growing to create the problem.

