>on this:

I don't know what Compaq's opinion on supported vs. unsupported is
these days (to the extent a corporation can have an opinion), but
when I was working at the CSC it went like this...

The Software Product Description for a particular version of an
operating system indicates what devices are supported.  Anything
not listed as supported is unsupported.  Digital could (probably)
not be held responsible for anything that happened to your system
as a consequence of using an unsupported device.

Unfortunately, "unsupported" was never qualified.  The three usual
choices were:

1.  Untested with no knowledge of how it will behave.

2.  Tested and someone has knowledge of how it misbehaves.

3.  Latent support in the operating system with the intent to
    support it eventually.  However, the qualification wasn't
    completed at the time the SPD was published and there might
    have been problems lurking.

A misbehaving device could do anything from having an annoying
delay in doing whatever it does, to hanging the bus or system
or corrupting the data as it is written.

The bottom line is that if you used an unsupported device, you
put your system at risk and that risk was wholey yours.  The
advantage of supported devices and controllers is that they
have been qualified and most or all of the problems found.

