In article <79ne55$ctv$1@newsflash.concordia.ca>, syl@alcor.concordia.ca (Sylvain Robitaille) writes:
|> The plan is simple: I would like to be able to apply a packet filter to
|> IP packets destined for a particular port on the host. There is only one
|> network adapter on the host. I would have preferred to use something
|> like tcp_wrappers, but the service I'm concerned about is not wrappable,
|> and it is commercial software, so I can't modify it to use libwrap.
|> 
|> The use of a firewall has been considered, but this *is* a university,
|> so I need something with a finer granularity, (thus the idea of
|> packet-filtering on the host).
|> 
|> I know it's possible to do, but I don't see any evidence that anyone's
|> doing this, either on this newsgroup or on the alpha-osf-managers
|> mailing list. I've reviewed the /usr/examples/packetfilters/pfsamp.c
|> file, as well as /usr/include/net/pfilt.h, but this still isn't really
|> clear to me.

Actually, it's not possible to do.  (I should point that I wrote
a large chunk of the packetfilter code in the kernel, a long time
ago.)

The packetfilter is specifically designed NOT to disturb packets
that would normally go to a port on the local host.

If you want to set up the system as a firewall, you could use
the "screend" facility (which I also designed).  But this also
will not affect packet delivery to ports on the local host.

And apparently you cannot use tcp_wrappers, so I think you
are pretty much out of luck, for now.

Sorry,
-Jeff



