In article <36B93259.1034F36A@Excell.Net>, "Stanley R. Shaw III" <Stan.Shaw@Excell.Net> writes:

>My / partition is to be filling up. I have not found any core dumps, and
>believe all my log files are on other partitions.  Is there a simple way
>to identify a "growing" file?

Look in /tmp to see if anything there is growing.  Look in /dev to
see if there are any files with names that look like device names,
but aren't special files.  This often happens with tape utilities;
someone using /dev/nrmtoh instead of /dev/nrmt0h.  Find a program
that will show open files, since many files in /tmp have had their
name deleted.  You can't see them, but they take space until they're
closed.

>Would there be any problem in moving my /sbin directory to another disk
>and using a symbolic link to access it?

Lots of problems.  For example, since mount is in /sbin, how would
you mount the other file system?  Better to move /tmp to its own
file system and/or make / bigger, than to try and figure what
else can be safely moved.

>
