I find it hard to believe any vendor really wants a vendor-neutral
product name.  You want the UNIX with the black block lettering on a
white label?  Might as well call it eunichs and get it over with.

In the UNIX market a vendor has to accomplish two conflicting goals: 
convince the customer that thier UNIX is "real UNIX" the same as any other 
UNIX (IMHO DEC did good when they changed from OSF/1 to Digital UNIX),
and convince you thier UNIX is better than any other UNIX.

When I was working on an ULTRIX/MIPS system I had a customer who felt
that "real UNIX" came from Sun or HP.  I assume he had never heard of
the early BSD or AT&T implementations on VAXen.

