Morten Knudsen wrote:

> have / and /usr partitions on two different advfs domains.
> Can I just create new partitions and use tar or something?
> I'm not sure what actually makes a DU/Alpha system boot.
>

 Let's consider you duplicate on rz2. first, you must have advfs boot
blocks :

disklabel -wr -t advfs rz2 RZ28
disklabel -er rz2                 # to get the desired size for /, /usr
[/var], and swap

then create the new domains and filesets
mkfdmn /dev/rz2a rd
mkfdmn /dev/rz2g ud

mkfset rd root
mkfset ud usr

mkdir /new_root   /new_usr
mount rd#root /new_root
mount ud#usr /new_usr


you can now check that boot blocks are OK :
/sbin/advfs/advscan -a -g          # volume must be bootable

then copy your system :

vdump 0f - / | (cd /new_root ; vrestore xf -)
vdump 0f - /usr | (cd /new_usr ; vrestore xf -)

now you must patch your new etc/fdmns hierarchy
assuming the 2 domains in your old system are root_domain and usr_domain
:


cd /new_root/etc/fdmns
mv root_domain old_root
mv usr_domain old_usr
mv rd root_domain
mv ud usr_domain

now you must patch /new_root/etc/fstab to point to the future new swap
partition (file system type "sw")

And at last, do not forget this one : if you use eager mode for
swapping, change the
sym. link /new_root/sbin/swapdefault to the new 1st swap partition

after you shutdown

>>> set bootdef_dev dka200
>>> boot

Enjoy

this is unused but edit /etc/rc.config in your new system to reflect the
correct swaping, you'll see
