Whoever burned-in that CD didn't do the job right. Or shall we say, used
inappropriate utility under Win95? Obviously, the program that created the CD
recognizes ISO-9960, but not Win95 long names. There's nothing you can do about
it.

> that lines in textfiles have a special character (is displayed as ^M in vi) at
> the end.

This DOS/UNIX incompatibility. UNIX uses LF for newline and DOS uses CR-LF
combination. That is why you see ^M (CR) at the end of every line. There should
be utility unix2dos/dos2unix and I think it is included in:

- MTOOLS (MS-DOS Tools)
- SOFTPC (PC Emulator for UNIX)

